# Offline mode does NOT read .env or database/migration environment settings.
# Use -source-snapshot <file> AND -destination-snapshot <file>, not with -snapshot.
# Default is compare-only; add -migration-out <file> to generate SQL offline.
# No SQL connection or execution; securely transfer snapshot.json only, not .env.
#
# Copy this file to .env in the directory from which you run the program.
# Save as UTF-8. Existing environment variables override these values.
# Single quotes preserve literal $, # and backslashes in connection strings.
# Replace hosts, databases, users and passwords with your own settings.
# Keep .env private; never commit real credentials.

MSSQL_SOURCE_DSN='server=SOURCE_HOST;database=SourceDB;user id=compare_user;password=CHANGE_ME;encrypt=true;TrustServerCertificate=false'
MSSQL_DESTINATION_DSN='server=DESTINATION_HOST;database=DestinationDB;user id=compare_user;password=CHANGE_ME;encrypt=true;TrustServerCertificate=false'

# Windows authentication: omit user id and password to use the current account.
# MSSQL_SOURCE_DSN='server=HOST\INSTANCE;database=SourceDB;encrypt=true;TrustServerCertificate=false'

# Optional online migration generation; no SQL is executed automatically.
# Supports SQL procedures/views/functions and synonyms, not table migrations.
# CLR functions are compared, but assembly/function changes require manual migration.
# Creates missing objects, alters changed modules and replaces changed synonyms.
# Destination-only objects are retained; no automatic cleanup/DROP of extras.
# Required destination schemas and compatible table prerequisites must exist.
# Disabled leaves any previously generated SQL file untouched.
MSSQL_GENERATE_MIGRATION=false
MSSQL_MIGRATION_OUT='migration.sql'
#
# Uses catalog dependencies, not alphabetical type order. Dynamic SQL needs review.
# Cycles, unresolved/cross-database references, schema-bound dependents and unsafe
# changes fail generation without replacing existing report/migration files.
# Generation or -snapshot -include-migration requires SELECT on
# sys.sql_expression_dependencies in addition to VIEW DEFINITION.
# Run the generated file on the recorded destination database/server, with no
# transaction already open. Review and test on a copy before production use.

# Output format, SQL mode, timeout and output path remain command-line options:
# .\dbcp.exe -sql-mode normalized -format html -out diff.html
#
# Track this source database against its last saved schema (destination ignored):
# .\dbcp.exe -snapshot -sql-mode normalized -format html
# Requires MSSQL_GENERATE_MIGRATION=false. Saves data/ddmmyyhhmmss/database-id/.
# Add -include-migration to capture catalog dependencies and safety metadata:
# .\dbcp.exe -snapshot -include-migration -sql-mode normalized -format html
# Still source-only, MSSQL_GENERATE_MIGRATION=false, no SQL generation/execution.
#
# On BOTH isolated networks, export with -snapshot -include-migration and the
# local database's MSSQL_SOURCE_DSN, even for the future destination snapshot.
# Transfer only JSON through an authorized secure channel to data/offline/ as
# source.json and destination.json. SQL definitions may contain secrets.
# Compare on a third machine without .env or credentials:
# .\dbcp.exe -source-snapshot data/offline/source.json -destination-snapshot data/offline/destination.json -format html -out data/offline/diff.html
# Explicitly generate a script from the same files without config or connection:
# .\dbcp.exe -source-snapshot data/offline/source.json -destination-snapshot data/offline/destination.json -format html -out data/offline/diff.html -migration-out data/offline/migration.sql
# Both snapshots need migration metadata; v1/compare-only snapshots remain
# comparable but must be re-exported with -include-migration for SQL generation.
# Missing/unsafe dependencies abort planning without replacing report/SQL.
# Report/SQL paths must differ from each other, both inputs and .env.
# Offline mode does not advance history and never executes the script.
# Review manually: destination may have drifted since capture; exact server/db
# guards are not a full drift check. Tables and CLR migrations remain manual.
